Here is a fourth case scenario focusing on a common cardiovascular-induced Adverse Drug Reaction, ideal for your Task 1 walkthrough:

Patient Details

Patient: 58-year-old male

Chief Complaint: Persistent, dry, tickling cough that worsens at night, causing sleep disturbance.

Relevant Medical History: Type 2 Diabetes, Essential Hypertension, non-smoker.

Timeline & Medication History

Month 1: Diagnosed with stage 1 hypertension. Prescribed Enalapril 10 mg once daily.

Month 2: Blood pressure successfully controlled (122/78 mmHg). Patient remains compliant with Enalapril and Metformin.

Month 3: Patient develops a dry, non-productive cough with a tickling sensation in the throat. No fever, sore throat, congestion, or shortness of breath.

Month 4: Cough persists despite over-the-counter cough suppressants. Patient visits the clinic to rule out a chest infection.

Intervention: Chest X-ray and lung exam are entirely normal. Enalapril is discontinued and substituted with Losartan 50 mg daily.

Follow-up (2 Weeks): The cough completely disappears within 10 days of stopping Enalapril.

Case Analysis Breakdown

Suspected Reaction: ACE Inhibitor-Induced Bradykinin Cough.

Suspected Drug: Enalapril.

Causality Rationale:

Pharmacological Mechanism: ACE inhibitors prevent the degradation of bradykinin and substance P in the lungs, leading to local irritation and triggering a dry cough response.

Temporal Relationship: Cough began approximately two months after initiating Enalapril, which fits the typical onset window (weeks to months post-treatment start).

De-challenge & Re-challenge: Complete resolution occurred shortly after stopping Enalapril. Switching to an ARB (Losartan), which does not affect bradykinin metabolism, kept blood pressure controlled without recurring cough.

Alternative Causes: Normal chest X-ray and absence of fever/infection rule out respiratory tract infections, asthma, or heart failure.
